course: english
lesson: 39
title: Planning before writing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A plan is a map you draw before a journey: a few words or pictures that show where your writing starts, where it goes and how it ends, so you don't get lost halfway.
explain: Josh started his lost-dog story before the teacher finished talking; ten minutes later the dog was stuck in a cupboard and Josh had no idea how to get it out, because he had never decided on an ending.
explain: Stories often use a story mountain: the opening at the bottom, the build-up climbing, the problem at the top, then the resolution and the ending on the way down; information texts suit a mind map, with the topic in the middle and a branch for each paragraph.
explain: A plan should be quick: three or four words for each part are enough, and small children can plan in pictures, called a storyboard; a beautiful colour-coded plan that eats 30 minutes of a 45-minute test leaves no time to write.
explain: Every plan starts with two questions: who will read this, and why am I writing it? A letter to the council needs a different voice from a note to your best mate.
fact: Planning means deciding what you will write, and in what order, before you start.
fact: Without a plan, writers often run out of ideas in the middle or cannot find an ending.
fact: A story mountain shows a common story shape: opening, build-up, problem, resolution and ending.
fact: A mind map puts the topic in the middle with a branch for each idea; each branch can become a paragraph of a non-fiction text.
fact: A storyboard is a plan made of pictures in boxes, like a comic; it suits young writers and anyone who thinks in pictures, and film makers plan their films with storyboards too.
fact: In a timed task, the plan must be short: a few minutes and a few words per part, and a few minutes at the end are saved for checking.
fact: Every piece of writing has an audience (who reads it) and a purpose (why it is written), and these change the words you choose.
fact: A plan can change while you write; it is a guide, not a cage.
fact: Many schools now ask to see the plan, because it shows the writer's thinking.
fact: In Scotland "jist" means just and "hen" is a friendly word for a girl or woman; in the American South "fixin' to" means about to.
fact: To "vibe" in Gen Z and Gen Alpha slang means to go with the feeling, without a plan.
exercise: Josh starts his story before the teacher finishes and gets stuck with the dog in a cupboard. What went wrong? | He never planned how the story would end | He used too many full stops in the opening | His story was about a dog, which is too hard | He wrote too slowly and neatly
exercise: Miss Khan draws a mountain on the board. What goes at the very top? | The problem, the most exciting moment | The opening, where the characters are met | The ending, when everyone goes home | The title, in big letters
exercise: Put the story mountain in order. | Opening, build-up, problem, resolution, ending | Problem, opening, ending, build-up, resolution | Ending, problem, opening, resolution, build-up | Build-up, ending, opening, problem, resolution
exercise: Isla's dad in Paisley says "Jist write the thing, hen, ye're wastin' time wi' boxes and arrows." What does "hen" mean? | A friendly word for a girl | A chicken kept on a farm | Someone who is very lazy | A clever pupil in her class
exercise: Why does Isla's teacher still want to see the boxes and arrows? | It shows her thinking and keeps the story on track | Plans are given far more marks than the story itself | Boxes and arrows look very nice on the classroom wall | Isla must practise drawing straight arrows with a ruler
exercise: Ella spends 30 of her 45 minutes on a colour-coded plan and writes half a page. What is the lesson? | In a timed task, keep the plan short | Plans should always take most of the time | Colours make writing slower to read | She should never plan again at all
exercise: Kai plans a report on sharks with "sharks" in the middle and branches for food, body, home and myths. What is this called? | A mind map | A story mountain | A storyboard | A timeline
exercise: What can each branch of Kai's mind map become? | A paragraph | A title | A full stop | A chapter title only
exercise: Tyler in Texas says "I'm fixin' to plan my story real quick." What does "fixin' to" mean? | About to | Repairing | Refusing to | Finished
exercise: The teacher asks "Who is going to read this letter?" Liam says "You, Miss." Why does she say "pretend it's the council"? | The audience changes how formal it should be | The council marks all of the school's homework every week | Every letter in school must always be sent to the council | She doesn't want to read any more letters herself
exercise: Grace says "I don't plan, I just vibe, it's more creative." What is the wisest answer? | A plan gives shape and still leaves room for surprises | Vibing always makes the best and most creative stories | Planning stops all creativity, so it is a waste of time | Only teachers need to plan, not pupils who write
exercise: Ali, 6, draws a boy, a kite, a tree, the kite stuck, then Dad with a ladder. What kind of plan is this? | A storyboard | A mind map | A paragraph | A list poem about kites
exercise: Gran in Belfast says "In my day ye wrote the story and that was that." Why do schools teach planning now? | It makes the thinking visible and helps writers | Children today are less clever than they were before | Planning is needed for good spelling | It is a new law passed by the government
exercise: How much should you write for each part of a quick plan? | A few words | A full page | Nothing at all | Every sentence
exercise: What are the audience and purpose of a piece of writing? | Who reads it and why it is written | How long it is and how neat the handwriting is | Which pen and paper it uses | The title and the last word
exercise: Halfway through, Freya gets a better idea than her plan. What should she do? | Change the plan; it is a guide, not a cage | Stop writing and start the whole story again from nothing | Ignore the idea because plans are fixed | Rub out the whole plan in a panic
test: Your mate goes "planning is for people who can't write, mate." What is the truth? | Strong writers plan so their writing has a shape | Only weak writers who can't write ever need a plan | Plans make writing worse every single time you use them | Planning is only for maths
test: ★★★ A 40-minute test: which plan of time is most sensible? | 5 minutes planning, 30 writing, 5 checking | 30 minutes planning, 10 writing | 0 planning, 40 writing, no checking | 20 planning, 20 checking, no writing
test: In a cartoon, a knight sets off on a quest with no map and rides in circles for years. What does this show about writing? | Without a plan you can go round in circles | Knights in armour cannot write any stories well | Maps are only useful for real journeys on horses | Riding a horse is always better than writing
test: What would happen if Kai used a story mountain for his shark facts? | It would fit badly; facts suit a mind map | It would be the perfect plan for any set of facts | The sharks would have to fight a big battle in it | Nothing, because all plans are exactly the same
test: ★★★ Grace's story ended "and then they all died, the end." Which part of the story mountain did she skip? | The resolution | The opening | The build-up | The title
test: Your sister, bit sarky: "Wow, a plan. Did a robot write your homework?" What is the best reply? | Planning is how writers think before writing | Yes, only robots ever write plans for their homework | Plans are banned at home by the school rules | Only robots and computers ever plan things
test: A boy in Sydney: "Gonna smash this story out this arvo, no plan." ("Arvo" is afternoon.) What is the risk? | He may get stuck or have no ending | Afternoons are bad for writing | He will write too much | Nothing, plans never help
test: ★★★ Which plan fits a letter to a head teacher asking for a longer break? | Reason 1, reason 2, reason 3, polite request | Opening, monster attack, escape, ending | Character, setting, dragon, twist | Rhyme, rhythm, verse, chorus
test: A Gen Alpha kid says "storyboards are just comics, they're mid." ("Mid" means not very good.) What is true? | Storyboards are real plans used by film makers too | Storyboards are only for babies who can't write yet | Comics can never help anyone with their writing | Storyboards are only used in maths lessons at school
test: Which question should come first when planning a piece of writing? | Who is it for, and why? | What colour pen do I use? | How many pages can I fill? | What is my favourite word?
test: A lad from Leeds says "Me plan's well short, three words a box." ("Well" means very.) Is that fine? | Yes, a short plan is enough | No, every box needs a page | No, plans must be in sentences | Yes, but only if the boxes are blue
exam: Identify the principal purpose of planning prior to composition. | To determine content and order before writing | To decorate the page prior to its submission for marking | To lengthen considerably the time expended on a task | To eliminate entirely the need for any subsequent writing
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a story mountain? | Opening, build-up, problem, resolution and ending | A list of facts arranged in alphabetical order | A diagram of the rhymes in a poem | A table of tenses for each verb
exam: Select the planning format most appropriate in respect of a non-chronological report. | A mind map with a branch for each sub-topic | A story mountain culminating in a dramatic climax | A sequence of rhyming couplets arranged by stanza | A single uninterrupted sentence of considerable length
exam: Ascertain the two considerations, hereinafter termed audience and purpose, that ought to precede any plan. | Who is to read the text, and why it is written | Which pen is used, and how neatly | How many words, and how many pages | Which title, and which font
